### 9.2 Reusable formulas

Let:

* `R` \= net product revenue after removing VAT where applicable;
* `B` \= bread transfer cost;
* `F` \= fillings;
* `S` \= sauces/garnishes;
* `P` \= packaging;
* `L` \= direct labour;
* `W` \= waste allowance;
* `U` \= incremental utilities;
* `C` \= platform commission and channel fees;
* `D` \= merchant-funded discounts;
* `Y` \= delivery subsidy;
* `X` \= refunds/credits allowance.

Then:

`Contribution = R - B - F - S - P - L - W - U - C - D - Y - X`

`Contribution margin % = Contribution / R`

`Break-even orders per period = Incremental fixed pilot cost / Average contribution per order`

For a target contribution rate `t`, percentage channel/discount fees `f`, and absolute variable costs `A`:

`Minimum revenue = A / (1 - f - t)`

This equation is only useful when all cost categories are included and `f + t` is below 100%.

### 9.3 Bread transfer cost

Bread should not enter the sandwich model at zero cost merely because it was produced in the same bakery. Its transfer cost should include:

* ingredients;
* direct bakery labour allocation;
* packaging or slicing loss where relevant;
* yield loss;
* a reasonable share of incremental utilities.

For early decisions, a standard cost per usable sandwich portion is adequate. Later, actual batch yield should replace the standard.
